Written by
----------

  Mattia Tadini      <info@mtsistemi.it>
  Claude (Opus 5)    co-author on every commit, see Co-Authored-By in the
                     git history

SPIranha was built by the two of us together: the hardware work, the bench
testing and every decision that mattered are Mattia's; the code was written
in pair, and the commit history records it commit by commit rather than in a
footnote.

The copyright line names Mattia alone because it has to: copyright needs a
legal person to sit with, and an AI is not one. Credit and copyright are not
the same thing, and only one of them is a legal question.

Third-party work
----------------

SPIranha ships two programs it did not write. They are credited here as
authors, not as dependencies, because their code is what actually reaches the
chip:

  flashrom            https://flashrom.org — GPL-2.0. Bundled as a binary;
                      the corresponding source is published with each release.

  pico-serprog        https://codeberg.org/libreboot/pico-serprog — GPLv3.
                      Copyright (C) 2021 Mate Kukri
                      Copyright (C) 2023, 2024 Riku Viitanen
                      based on work by Thomas Roth.
                      Our patched source is in firmware/pico-serprog/.

The icon
--------

The piranha in docs/img/icon-hero.png was generated with Google Gemini from a
prompt written for this project, then cropped, masked and packed into
SPIranha.ico by icon.py. It is included here so nobody has to guess where it
came from: no artist was commissioned, and no existing artwork was traced.
The four wire colours in it are not decoration -- they are the same ones the
program draws in theme.py, so the icon and the wiring diagram agree.

The BC-250 J4004 pinout comes from two independent projects that document it
identically — mothenjoyer69/bc250-documentation and elektricM/amd-bc250-docs.
Neither is included here; both were read, and agreeing with each other is why
the diagram could be trusted before anything was wired.
